Only flag real IRAP NALs as key frames (Qualcomm HEVC encoder, Issue 104).

Qualcomm's c2.qti.hevc.encoder (seen on the Galaxy S24 Ultra, SM8650) sets
BUFFER_FLAG_KEY_FRAME on every periodic "sync frame" it emits, but for the
lifetime of one encode only ever writes a single true IDR NAL: every later
"sync frame" is coded as an ordinary TRAIL_R picture that happens to be
intra. A 25 s H.265 compression confirmed this on-device: ffprobe's packet
flags and the encoder's own BufferInfo both call out 25 "key" packets (one
a second, matching -g/sync-frame-interval), but `trace_headers` on the same
file shows exactly one NAL of type 19 (IDR_W_RADL) for the whole file; the
other 24 are type 1 (TRAIL_R).

The GOP structure the platform exposes to force real periodic IDRs
(coding.gop) is a vector-typed C2 parameter; FFmpeg's generic AMediaFormat
configuration cannot set it (logcat: "ignored struct field
coding.gop.values"), so passing -g cannot make this encoder produce more
than one IDR. That part is not fixed here — see "Known limitation" below.

mediacodec_receive() trusted BufferInfo's key-frame flag unconditionally,
so the container's seek table ends up with 24 "sync points" that are not
random-access points. A player that seeks to one of them flushes its
decoder and feeds it a picture with no reference context to decode from.
That plausibly explains the reported MKV/MOV seek freeze, and — since a
resumed player likely re-seeks to its last position the same way — the
black screen after returning from Settings, but the second half is not
independently confirmed.

This patch adds mce_packet_has_irap_nal(), which walks the Annex B NALs of
the finished packet and checks the real HEVC NAL type (BLA/IDR/CRA, types
16-23), and only sets AV_PKT_FLAG_KEY when the codec's flag and the
bitstream agree. It is HEVC-only: the bug was only ever observed on this
hardware HEVC encoder, and gating the H.264 path on the same check as well
would be an unverified, broader behavior change affecting every AVC
encoder's output, including the common default path.

This does not make the encoder produce more IDRs — it only stops the
container lying about where the existing ones are, so a seek decodes from
the one real IDR instead of handing the decoder a picture it cannot
resync from. That is slower than a seek near the target, but it finishes
instead of sticking.

Known limitation: with only this patch, every long HEVC compression on
this encoder still has exactly one real seek point, at t=0 — the same
shape as the AV1/VP9 single-keyframe regression fixed for Issues 106-108
(video.go:582-593), except there the fix was `-g`; here `-g` already
reaches the encoder (confirmed: it sets "coding.sync-frame-interval" and
produces a real periodic intra picture every second) and still does not
produce more than one IDR, because turning those periodic intra pictures
into IDRs needs the vector-typed coding.gop parameter FFmpeg cannot set.
On a short clip (the 25 s test file) decoding from t=0 is fast enough not
to be noticeable; on a long one it would not be. Making the encoder emit
real periodic IDRs needs either a vendor-specific GOP parameter this
patch does not attempt to guess, or a per-GOP request-sync-frame call
(coding.request-sync-frame is visible in this device's own CCodecConfig
dump), and either way needs verification on the S24 Ultra, which this
patch has not had.

The call site is patched in place inside mediacodec_receive(); the helper
itself is defined afterward, right after mediacodec_receive() ends and
before mediacodec_get_input_index() begins, with a forward declaration
added next to mediacodec_generate_extradata()'s existing one near the top
of the file. Both insertion points sit in gaps 0001 leaves untouched
(0001-libavcodec-mediacodecenc-Normalize-vendor-CSD-single-param-snapshot.patch
is one large contiguous insertion ending partway into mediacodec_receive's
body, followed by three more hunks inside that same function): landing
new code inside any of those hunks, even between two of 0001's own
functions, breaks the exact contiguous-text match the re-entrant
"already applied" reverse-check and a future reverse-apply rely on. This
patch's own hunks each sit entirely in pristine code 0001 never touches,
so applying it does not disturb 0001's re-entrancy, and reversing 0002
alone (checked with `git apply --check --reverse`) cleanly undoes it
without touching 0001.

